{"name":"Wild Thyme","hex":"#7e9c6f","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"wild thyme","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A flowering plant in the mint family, of species Thymus serpyllum."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wild_thyme"}],"entries":[{"title":"Thymus serpyllum","blocks":[{"text":"Thymus serpyllum, known by the common names of Breckland thyme, Breckland wild thyme, wild thyme, creeping thyme, or elfin thyme, is a species of flowering plant in the mint family, Lamiaceae. It is a low, usually prostrate subshrub forming creeping stems up to 10 cm (4 in) tall. The oval evergreen leaves are up to 8 mm. The strongly scented flowers are either lilac, pink-purple, or magenta, are up to 6 mm long and produced in clusters."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Thymus_serpyllum1.jpg/330px-Thymus_serpyllum1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thymus_serpyllum"},{"title":"Thyme","blocks":[{"text":"Thyme is a culinary herb consisting of the dried aerial parts of some members of the genus Thymus of flowering plants in the mint family Lamiaceae. Thymes are native to Mediterranean countries in North Africa, Levant and Southern Europe. Thymes have culinary, medicinal, and ornamental uses. The species most commonly cultivated and used for culinary purposes is Thymus vulgaris, native to Southeast Europe."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Thyme-Bundle.jpg/330px-Thyme-Bundle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thyme"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Thymus_serpyllum_flowering_plants.jpg/120px-Thymus_serpyllum_flowering_plants.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Thymus_serpyllu